.TH "TRACEE\-REPLAY" "1" "2026/07" "" "Tracee Replay Command Manual"
.SS NAME
tracee \f[B]replay\f[R] \- Replay past events from a file and process
them with detectors
.SS SYNOPSIS
tracee \f[B]replay\f[R] <file> options
.SS DESCRIPTION
The \f[B]replay\f[R] command allows you to replay past events from a
file and process them with detectors.
This is useful for analyzing historical event data, testing detector
configurations, and debugging detector behavior.
.PP
\f[B]Important\f[R]: Only low\-level events should be replayed.
Detector events (high\-level events) are automatically filtered out
during replay.
.SS ARGUMENTS
.TP
\f[B]file\f[R]
Path to the event file to replay.
The file should contain events in JSON Lines format (one JSON event per
line), as produced by tracee with \f[CR]\-\-output json:file.json\f[R].
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-\-output\f[R], \f[B]\-o\f[R] <format>[:path]
Control how and where output is printed.
Format can be \f[CR]json\f[R], \f[CR]table\f[R], \f[CR]webhook\f[R],
etc.
Path is optional and defaults to \f[CR]stdout\f[R] for most formats.
.PP
\f[B]Note\f[R]: Replay mode currently supports a single output
destination at a time.
Configuring multiple output streams or destinations is rejected with an
error.
.PP
Examples: \- \f[CR]\-\-output json:stdout\f[R] \- JSON format to stdout
\- \f[CR]\-\-output table\f[R] \- Table format to stdout \-
\f[CR]\-\-output json:output.json\f[R] \- JSON format to file \-
\f[CR]\-\-output webhook:http://localhost:8080\f[R] \- Send to webhook
.TP
\f[B]\-\-detectors\f[R] [path\&...]
Configure YAML detector search directories or files.
Can be specified multiple times to search multiple directories or files.
.PP
Examples: \- \f[CR]\-\-detectors /etc/tracee/detectors\f[R] \-
\f[CR]\-\-detectors /path/to/detectors \-\-detectors /another/path\f[R]
\- \f[CR]\-\-detectors /path/to/detector.yaml\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-logging\f[R], \f[B]\-l\f[R] <option>
Logger options.
Use \f[CR]level=<level>\f[R] where level is \f[CR]debug\f[R],
\f[CR]info\f[R], \f[CR]warn\f[R], \f[CR]error\f[R] or \f[CR]fatal\f[R]
(default \f[CR]info\f[R]), and \f[CR]file=<path>\f[R] to write logs to a
file (default stderr).
.PP
Examples: \- \f[CR]\-\-logging level=debug\f[R] \- Debug level logging
\-
\f[CR]\-\-logging level=info \-\-logging file=/var/log/tracee.log\f[R]
\- Info level to a file
.SS ARCHITECTURE
.SS Event Filtering
The replay command automatically filters out detector events
(high\-level events) from the input file.
Only low\-level events are replayed.
.SS EXAMPLES
.SS Basic Replay
Replay events from a file with default settings:
.IP
.EX
tracee replay events.json
.EE
.SS Replay with Table Output
Replay events and display results in table format:
.IP
.EX
tracee replay events.json \-\-output table
.EE
.SS Replay with Custom Detectors
Replay events using detectors from a custom directory:
.IP
.EX
tracee replay events.json \-\-detectors /path/to/detectors
.EE
.SS Replay with Debug Logging
Replay events with debug\-level logging:
.IP
.EX
tracee replay events.json \-\-logging level=debug
.EE
.SS Complete Example
Capture events, then replay them:
.IP
.EX
# Capture events to file
tracee \-\-events execve,openat \-\-output json:events.json

# Replay events with detectors
tracee replay events.json \-\-output table \-\-detectors /etc/tracee/detectors \-\-logging level=info
.EE
.SS FILE FORMAT
The replay file should contain events in JSON Lines format (one JSON
event per line).
Each line should be a valid JSON object representing a
\f[CR]v1beta1.Event\f[R] protobuf message in JSON format.
.PP
Events are dispatched by the numeric \f[CR]id\f[R] (the protobuf
\f[CR]EventId\f[R] enum value, e.g.\ \f[CR]60\f[R] for
\f[CR]execve\f[R], \f[CR]258\f[R] for \f[CR]openat\f[R]); the
\f[CR]name\f[R] field is informational.
.PP
Example file content:
.IP
.EX
{\[dq]timestamp\[dq]:\[dq]2024\-01\-01T00:00:00Z\[dq],\[dq]id\[dq]:60,\[dq]name\[dq]:\[dq]execve\[dq],\[dq]workload\[dq]:{\[dq]process\[dq]:{\[dq]executable\[dq]:{\[dq]path\[dq]:\[dq]/bin/bash\[dq]}}}}
{\[dq]timestamp\[dq]:\[dq]2024\-01\-01T00:00:01Z\[dq],\[dq]id\[dq]:258,\[dq]name\[dq]:\[dq]openat\[dq],\[dq]workload\[dq]:{\[dq]process\[dq]:{\[dq]executable\[dq]:{\[dq]path\[dq]:\[dq]/bin/bash\[dq]}}}}
.EE
.SS NOTES
.IP \[bu] 2
Detector events in the input file are automatically filtered out
.IP \[bu] 2
Only low\-level events are processed
.IP \[bu] 2
Events are processed in file order
